# Single source of truth for the benchmark Matrix account and homeserver.
#
# Sourced by the homeserver scripts, by the bot, by every client's seed script
# and by the smoke test.  If you change the user or domain, reseed the server -
# room aliases and the seeded membership embed them.
#
# Every value is a DEFAULT: written as ${VAR:-...} so that anything already in
# the environment wins.  That is what makes `environment:` in a usage_scenario,
# a --build-arg, or an inline `PARROT_MATRIX_HISTORY=200 ./setup.sh` take
# effect.  Plain assignments here would silently override all three.

# The account under test.
PARROT_MATRIX_USER="${PARROT_MATRIX_USER:-parrot}"
PARROT_MATRIX_PASS="${PARROT_MATRIX_PASS:-parrot}"
PARROT_MATRIX_ID="${PARROT_MATRIX_ID:-@parrot:parrot.test}"
PARROT_MATRIX_DOMAIN="${PARROT_MATRIX_DOMAIN:-parrot.test}"

# The second human in the corpus - the one the scenario invites to the room it
# creates, and the sender of most of the seeded conversation.
PARROT_PEER_USER="${PARROT_PEER_USER:-alice}"
PARROT_PEER_ID="${PARROT_PEER_ID:-@alice:parrot.test}"

# The bot behind the `ping` and `drip` triggers.  See matrixserver/parrot-bot.py
# for why the scenario has to trigger the server rather than the other way round.
PARROT_BOT_USER="${PARROT_BOT_USER:-echo}"
PARROT_BOT_PASS="${PARROT_BOT_PASS:-echo}"
PARROT_BOT_ID="${PARROT_BOT_ID:-@echo:parrot.test}"

# Hostname of the homeserver container.  This resolves because every
# usage_scenario.yml declares it as a Docker network alias on the synapse
# service, so the name a client connects to matches the server_name Synapse was
# built with.
PARROT_MATRIX_HOST="${PARROT_MATRIX_HOST:-matrix.parrot.test}"
PARROT_MATRIX_PORT="${PARROT_MATRIX_PORT:-8008}"

# What the clients and the bot connect to.
#
# Plain HTTP, and that is a deliberate compromise - the same one the email group
# makes with PARROT_MAIL_SECURITY=none.  It is the only setting every client can
# be pre-configured for without a human accepting a self-signed certificate
# mid-measurement, so it keeps the comparison about the client rather than about
# whose trust dialog appears when.  Some clients refuse plain HTTP homeservers
# without a config override; that override belongs in the client's seed script.
PARROT_MATRIX_URL="${PARROT_MATRIX_URL:-http://matrix.parrot.test:8008}"

# What the bot does when the scenario triggers it.  The scenario's last block is
# written around these two numbers: 24 messages five seconds apart puts the last
# one at t+120s, which is the "two minutes of messages arriving" the block
# claims to measure.  Change them and the block's wording and the recording's
# pacing both have to change with them.
PARROT_BOT_DRIP_COUNT="${PARROT_BOT_DRIP_COUNT:-24}"
PARROT_BOT_DRIP_INTERVAL="${PARROT_BOT_DRIP_INTERVAL:-5.0}"

# Depth of the seeded history in the busy room.
#
# 8000, cut down from 40000 after measuring what deep history actually buys. It
# is NOT what makes the initial sync expensive: Synapse's initial sync returns a
# bounded timeline per room - a few tens of events - plus the full room state,
# so the sync cost is driven by the MEMBER count and the ROOM count, both of
# which are unchanged. History depth only feeds the scroll-back block, which
# pages through ten screens: a few hundred messages.
#
# The difference is all build time. At 508 members every event fans out to every
# member's timeline, and seeding measured ~7 events/second on this machine:
# 40000 messages is about 95 minutes of build, 8000 about 19. 8000 still leaves
# roughly 25x headroom over what the scenario actually pages through.
PARROT_MATRIX_HISTORY="${PARROT_MATRIX_HISTORY:-8000}"
